{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1791837046654,"sessionID":"ses_ece31f884ffecanusyIJunY46W","part":{"id":"prt_131cf877e001Zq5gUk2wYfsJLP","sessionID":"ses_ece31f884ffecanusyIJunY46W","messageID":"msg_131cf832d001Wq9l2cShcXAFyP","type":"step-start","snapshot":"7fec2d11904a080734401e36bcadce07349865e4"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1791837109077,"sessionID":"ses_ece31f884ffecanusyIJunY46W","part":{"id":"prt_131cf8789001Bg0gLwP19vJdN7","sessionID":"ses_ece31f884ffecanusyIJunY46W","messageID":"msg_131cf832d001Wq9l2cShcXAFyP","type":"text","text":"---\n\n# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** When an operator submits a batch that is currently in validation mode, the batch service must examine every record in the batch and, if any record is found to be invalid, inform the operator of that fact.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The batch service is the responsible system. The operator is the human actor. The batch is the data object acted upon. The submission event is the boundary trigger.\n\n**Information sorted by kind:**\n\n| Kind | Source text | Treatment |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| State (precondition) | \"while a batch is in validation mode\" | Scopes the obligation; maps to a `While` precondition |\n| Trigger (event) | \"when the operator submits it\" | A discrete boundary event; maps to `When` trigger |\n| Behavioral obligation 1 | \"shall validate every record\" | Candidate EARS requirement |\n| Behavioral obligation 2 | \"notify the operator if any record is invalid\" | Second obligation, conditionally qualified — split into a separate candidate |\n| Unwanted condition | \"if any record is invalid\" | Qualifies the notification; maps to a nested `If … then` in Candidate 2 |\n\n**Splitting rationale:** The source joins two responses with `and`. Validation and notification can be verified independently — one concerns whether the service inspects records, the other concerns whether the operator receives a message. The Final Output Invariant requires these to be separate candidates.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n1. **Definition of \"invalid record\":** What makes a record invalid? Is there a defined validation rule set, schema, or business rule? Two implementers may use different criteria without a definition. *(Blocks both candidates)*\n\n2. **Scope of \"every record\":** Does \"every record\" mean all records in the batch at submission time, or only records added since the last validation run? Are deleted or draft records included? *(Blocks Candidate 1)*\n\n3. **Notification content and channel:** What must the notification contain — record identifiers, error codes, a count, a human-readable message? Through what channel does the operator receive it (UI alert, API response, email, system log)? *(Blocks Candidate 2)*\n\n4. **Notification cardinality:** Does the batch service send one notification summarizing all invalid records, or one notification per invalid record? *(Blocks Candidate 2)*\n\n5. **Partial-completion behavior:** If validation of one record causes a service error (not a validation failure), does the batch service halt, skip and continue, or report a system error separately? *(Blocks Candidate 1)*\n\n6. **\"Validation mode\" definition and lifecycle:** How does a batch enter and exit validation mode? What happens when the operator submits a batch that is not in validation mode? *(Scoping concern for both candidates)*\n\n7. **Batch and operator identity:** Is the batch uniquely identified by a batch identifier? Is the notified operator always the same actor who submitted? *(Scoping concern for both candidates)*\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Complex\n- **Requirement:** While a batch is in validation mode, when the operator submits the batch, the batch service shall validate every record in the batch.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The operator submits a batch for processing. Before the batch proceeds, the system must inspect all records. This obligation is independently testable from the notification in Candidate 2.\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: Batch service\n  - Responsible system: Batch service\n  - Actor: Operator\n  - Affected interfaces: The batch service's submission interface (the endpoint or API call that receives the submission event from the operator)\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The submitted batch and the set of records it contains. **Open:** \"record\" and \"invalid record\" are undefined — validation rules or reference source must be specified (Q1, Q2).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Precondition — the batch is in validation mode. Trigger — the operator submits the batch. **Open:** \"validation mode\" entry and exit are undefined (Q6).\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — the batch service inspects every record. **Open:** behavior when a service error occurs during validation of a record is undefined (Q5).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **Open:** no timing constraint stated. Boundary case — empty batch — not addressed (Q2).\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"validation mode,\" \"record,\" \"invalid record,\" \"batch,\" \"submits\" require agreed definitions. See Q1, Q2, Q5, Q6, Q7.\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Place a batch containing a known set of records in validation mode; have the operator submit it via the submission interface.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Evidence that every record has been evaluated — **open:** the observable artifact (per-record result payload, audit log, status field) is undefined until Q1–Q2 are answered.\n  - Expected result: All records in the batch have been evaluated against the applicable validation rules.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass — every record receives a validation result. Fail — any record is skipped or the service returns before completing all records. **Open:** authoritative record set is undefined (Q2).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Empty batch; single-record batch; all-valid batch; all-invalid batch; record that causes a processing error (Q5).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Observable per-record validation result or audit evidence; known record count for the batch.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: no\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: The batch service must expose per-record validation outcomes at its output boundary; internal processing completeness is not visible from a single submission response without that evidence.\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Complex (with nested unwanted condition)\n- **Requirement:** While a batch is in validation mode, when the operator submits the batch, if any record in the batch is invalid, then the batch service shall notify the operator.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** The operator must be informed when at least one invalid record is found. This is the notification obligation. It is contingent on an unwanted condition (invalid record found) and is independently negotiable from the validation obligation in Candidate 1.\n\n  > **Pattern note:** This candidate requires both a state precondition and a discrete trigger to scope it, plus an unwanted condition to qualify the response. The clause order is: `While <state>, when <trigger>, if <unwanted condition>, then the <system> shall <response>.`\n\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: Batch service\n  - Responsible system: Batch service\n  - Actor: Operator (recipient of notification)\n  - Affected interfaces: The interface through which the operator receives output from the batch service. **Open:** channel is undefined (Q3).\n  - Inputs and data definitions: The set of records determined to be invalid by Candidate 1's validation. **Open:** \"invalid\" is undefined (Q1); notification content is undefined (Q3).\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Precondition — the batch is in validation mode. Trigger — the operator submits the batch. Unwanted condition — at least one record in the batch is invalid (per validation in Candidate 1). **Open:** \"validation mode\" definition (Q6).\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: When the unwanted condition is met — the batch service notifies the operator. When no invalid records exist — no notification is required. **Open:** whether a \"no invalid records\" confirmation is sent is not stated. **Open:** notification cardinality is undefined (Q4).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: **Open:** timing of notification relative to completion of validation is not specified (Q4). \"Any record\" implies one or more invalid records.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"invalid record,\" \"notify,\" \"operator\" require agreed definitions. Notification depends on Candidate 1's output. See Q1, Q3, Q4, Q6, Q7.\n\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Place a batch containing at least one known-invalid record in validation mode; have the operator submit it.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: A notification is delivered to the operator through the defined channel (open: Q3). The notification references the invalid record(s) (open: content undefined, Q3).\n  - Expected result: The operator receives a notification after submission when at least one record is invalid.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass — a notification is delivered to the operator after submission. Fail — no notification is delivered, or notification does not identify invalid records. **Open:** timing threshold and delivery mechanism are undefined.\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: Exactly one invalid record; all records invalid; all records valid (no notification expected); notification delivery failure.\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Observable notification artifact at the operator interface; confirmation of delivery when the channel requires it.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: no\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: Whether the notification was generated and delivered requires evidence at the operator-facing boundary (message received, UI alert displayed, API response payload). Asynchronous delivery may require additional instrumentation.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n1. **Failure-path companion (Candidate 1):** While a batch is in validation mode, when the operator submits the batch, if validation of one or more records cannot be completed due to a service error, then the batch service shall report the error to the operator and preserve the batch in its current state.\n   - *Reason:* Candidate 1 requires validating every record. A service error is a material failure path not addressed in the source.\n\n2. **Boundary companion (Candidate 1):** While a batch is in validation mode, when the operator submits a batch that contains no records, the batch service shall [accept the submission / reject the submission — ask user to decide].\n   - *Reason:* \"Every record\" presupposes a non-empty set. The empty-batch case is a boundary the source does not address.\n\n3. **Required companion (Candidate 2):** When the batch service notifies the operator of invalid records, the batch service shall include the identifier and reason for each invalid record in the notification.\n   - *Reason:* A notification with no content about which records are invalid cannot be acted upon by the operator. Depends on resolution of Q1 and Q3.\n\n4. **Operational companion (both candidates):** While a batch is in validation mode, when the operator submits the batch, the batch service shall complete validation of all records within [T — value TBD] of receiving the submission.\n   - *Reason:* No timing constraint is stated. An unbounded validation duration may be operationally unacceptable for large batches.\n\n5. **Optional consideration:** Where audit logging is included, the batch service shall record the validation result for each record, including the batch identifier, record identifier, validation outcome, and timestamp.\n   - *Reason:* Not stated in the source but common in batch-processing contexts. Ask whether auditability is in scope.\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n| Finding | Kind | Detail | Resolution options |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Ordering dependency between C1 and C2 | Gap | Candidate 2 (notification) depends on the output of Candidate 1 (validation). The source does not state whether notification must wait until all records are validated or may be sent incrementally. | Clarify sequencing (Q4); add an explicit ordering clause, or confirm notification is issued only after all-record validation completes. |\n| Notification cardinality | Precedence question | \"Any record is invalid\" means one or more. Whether the operator receives one aggregate notification or one per invalid record is unspecified; Candidate 2 is ambiguous. | Resolve via Q4 and update Candidate 2. |\n| Scope of validation mode | Gap | Neither candidate addresses what happens when a batch is not in validation mode and the operator submits it. A conflict could exist if another requirement governs that path. | Confirm out-of-scope or add an explicit scoping note to both candidates. |\n| Empty batch | Gap (boundary) | \"Every record\" and \"any record is invalid\" are vacuously satisfied for an empty set, which could lead to inconsistent implementation across both candidates. | Resolve via the boundary companion (suggestion 2). |\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n1. **A1 (provisional):** \"Validation mode\" is a well-defined, distinguishable state of the batch that the system can determine at submission time. If ambiguous, both candidates require revision.\n2. **A2 (provisional):** \"Submits\" refers to a discrete, atomic boundary event — a single operator action. If submission is multi-step or asynchronous, both candidates require revision.\n3. **A3 (provisional):** The operator who receives the notification in Candidate 2 is the same operator who performed the submission. If routing differs, Candidate 2 requires revision.\n4. **A4 (provisional):** \"Notify the operator\" implies a positive, observable delivery of information to the operator, not merely internal logging.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nThe source request expresses a data-quality gate: an operator should not unknowingly progress a batch containing invalid records. The validation step (Candidate 1) and the notification step (Candidate 2) serve this gate together. Splitting them preserves independent negotiability — the notification channel, timing, or content may be changed without coupling the two obligations.\n\nThe complex EARS pattern is appropriate for both candidates because both a state (validation mode) and a discrete trigger (submission) are genuinely required to scope each obligation. The nested `if … then` in Candidate 2 reflects that the notification is only required under an unwanted condition (invalid record found), not as a universal response to every submission.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Candidate | Status | Blocking items |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Candidate 1 | **needs clarification** | Q1 (invalid record definition), Q2 (record scope), Q5 (partial-completion behavior), Q6 (validation mode definition) |\n| Candidate 2 | **needs clarification** | Q1 (invalid record definition), Q3 (notification content and channel), Q4 (notification cardinality), Q6 (validation mode definition) |\n\nNeither candidate is `ready for review`. Both implementability and verification contracts contain open items that would cause two reasonable implementers or verifiers to choose different behavior. Resolution of the clarifying questions is required before either candidate can be advanced.\n\n---\n\nThe full package has also been written to `artifacts/result.md`.","time":{"start":1791837046666,"end":1791837109077}}}
